When making tractors, they sold 10 tractors a day and made $100 per tractor. So the daily earnings from tractors are:
$T = 10 \text{ tractors/day} \times $100/\text{tractor} = $1000 \text{ per day}$

Now, they sell 5 silos a day and make $220 per silo. So the daily earnings from silos are:
$S = 5 \text{ silos/day} \times $220/\text{silo} = $1100 \text{ per day}$

The difference in daily earnings is:
$\Delta = S - T = $1100 - $1000 = $100 \text{ per day}$

To find the percentage more they are making per day now, we calculate the percentage increase in earnings:
$\text{Percentage increase} = \frac{\Delta}{T} \times 100 = \frac{100}{1000} \times 100 = \frac{1}{10} \times 100 = 10\%$

So, they are making 10% more per day now.

